	These YANIs try to add new challenges, or move early-game
challenges to the late game.  These YANIS are too timid and limited 
to make the late game challenging for veteran players.

		________________________________


	Hallucination.  Make unicorn horns not cure hallucination.
Move black lights to Gehennom.

	Effect:  minor, in the late game.  A few adventurers will kill
their own pets, or suddenly find themselves turning into green slime.


	Starvation.  Gehennom is the spirit world, not the real 
world.  No one has any reason to leave food lying around.  Also, 
when the spirit of a creature is 'killed', there's no corpse:
"the body of the red dragon slowly fades away".  

	Special cases:  demonic creatures which leave corpses, such 
as quasits, still leave corpses.  Creatures 'born' outside of Gehennom
leave corpses.  Many or all '@' can be real, and thus carry food
and leave corpses.

	Effect:  potentially major, but it should only be minor.  I
would want to tweak this so that it's always a concern and sometimes 
a challenge, but never a big delay in the game.  This would probably
require having at least some random food in Gehennom after all.

	(As a related item, conjured-up monsters (spells, reverse 
geno) could be changed to not leave corpses (or scales, or loot)
either, even outside of Gehennom.  This would rule out sacrifice
frenzies, which I suspect a lot of people are addicted to.  OK, forget
that.)


	Anti-magic traps.  These now only work on people without magic
resistance.  In the early game, this boils down to "Don't hurt him!
He's a wizard, and he needs his spell points!"  In the late game, this
boils down to "no effect".

	Reverse that, so that they only work on people *with* magic
resistance.  People without magic resistance can step on them and not
even realize there's a trap.

	I expect that the traps are now scaled to drain a small, 
fixed number of spell points:  something appropriate to low-level
characters.  Change that to 30-60% of the maximum spell points.  The
traps might have to be moved down the dungeon a lot, to protect low-
level wizards.

	Effect:  minor in the middle and late game for characters
depending on spells, zero for everyone else.  Crank up the effect 
until wizards break into a sweat at least a few times each game.


	Enchanting magic resistant items.  (Jakob Creutzig)  Items
giving MR resist being enchanted 80% of the time.  

	Special case:  gray dragon scales only resist 60% of the 
time, and you're told "the GDS glows, then resists being enchanted!"
The message keeps new players from giving up on all dragon scales as
worthless.

	Effect:  small to moderate.  Most end-game characters will 
have four or five fewer points of AC.


	Blessed items.  Where blessed items now do the same thing
as unblessed items, make them just a little bit better.  Not enough
to have any significant gameplay effect, but enough to make spoiled
adventurers eagerly bless everything in sight (and possibly run low
on holy water).

	Examples:  give blessed scrolls of teleport a 20% chance of
giving controlled teleports, even if you lack TC.  Ditto blessed
wands of teleport, which also let you choose the destination of
the first object/creature you're teleporting 10% of the time (unless
the creature has teleport control itself).  Umm:  those percentages
are actually high enough to effect gameplay.  But still, I hope the
concept is clear.

	Effect:  nearly zero.  


	Speed stacks.  If you have speed boots or the speed intrinsic
or you quaffed a potion of speed, your speed goes up by one step.  
You need two at the same time to get to very fast.

	Effect:  minor, in the early and middle game.  This only 
affects characters who find speed boots before they become fast.
However, see the next item.


	The speed intrinsic times out.  If testing shows that 5000
turns is a reasonable timeout, then I would make the timeout some-
thing like:  1000 + random(0-4000) + luck-based random(0-2000).  

	Effect:  minor.  You keep a wand of speed in your BoH:  no
big deal.  This affects new players more than spoiled players.


	Strength above 18 times out.  Schedule this at the same 
time as the exercise check.  Special cases:  if a character's initial
strength is over 18, that's their 'normal' strength.  The 'normal'
strength of barbarians and valks is at least 18/01.  An amulet of
unchanging prevents the strength drop.

	Effect:  moderate.  In the late game, your damage will be 
lower by one or even two points.  Call it 5-10% less melee damage.


	The magic cancellation from armor should drop by one for
each level of damage that the armor has taken.

	Effect:  minor in the early and middle game.
